WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.340
When it comes to bugs, the SCP Foundation
certainly has its hands full.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:11.890
You might remember SCP-726, a species of blowfly
aptly named the reconstructive maggots, who

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:16.020
recreated the bodies of other organisms through
horrible reproduction methods.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:21.900
There’s also SCP-2031, a colony of anomalous
ants that would eat their victim’s insides

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.670
and burrow themselves in the bodies, puppeting
them around and masquerading as the creature

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.859
they just ate, even if it was a person.

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:35.570
Today on SCP Explained, we’re going to be
talking about a new species of anomalous insect,

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.129
SCP-378, nicknamed the Brainworm.

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:42.129
Well, it’s an anomaly only new to us.

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:48.149
For the Foundation, SCP-378’s discovery
and containment dates back to the days of

00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:51.260
the American Secure Containment Initiative.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.590
The American Secure Containment Initiative
could be considered a predecessor to the SCP

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.230
Foundation, active for the majority of the
first half of the 20th century.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:06.040
While most of the stories go that the ASCI,
alongside several other paranormal investigation

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.229
organizations, eventually came together and
formed the SCP Foundation in the early 1950s,

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:13.750
the reality is a lot murkier.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:18.570
As we all know, it’s very hard to completely
kill off an organization in the anomalous

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:19.570
world.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.900
The secrecy and decentralization practically
ensures that there’s always going to be

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:30.479
some stragglers left, and in the ASCI’s
case, that certainly seems to be the case.

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:37.361
SCP-378’s file indicates that by 1963, the
ASCI was still around and kicking, though

00:01:37.361 --> 00:01:41.860
it was definitely living on borrowed time
due to the rising prominence of the SCP Foundation.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:48.600
The two organizations, the growing Foundation
and the dying ASCI, attempted to keep tabs

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.750
on one another throughout the decade, but
the Foundation’s espionage programs proved

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.040
to be more intensive and thorough.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.299
For whatever reason, the Foundation was always
getting the best intel, whether it was beating

00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:04.829
the ASCI to the punch on newly discovered
anomalies, gaining access to otherwise classified

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:10.680
information only available in ASCI documents
of the highest security clearances, or finding

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.890
ways to benefit from the organization’s
detriments through diplomatic meetings that

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.820
always seemed to work in the Foundation’s
favor, it was clear that the SCP Foundation

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:25.260
was the new hotness, and the ASCI would have
a lot of catching up to do if it wanted to

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.470
remain relevant in this new, post World War
2 world.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.850
Unfortunately, we all know what happened.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:37.080
The majority of the ASCI was absorbed into
the Foundation shortly after its formation,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.130
and the pieces that remained would fade into
obscurity in the coming years.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.100
But why was the Foundation’s intel so good?

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.280
More funding?

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.570
Better-trained agents?

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:47.570
Luck?

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:51.690
The answer, if you’re familiar with the
Foundation’s ways, shouldn’t come as a

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.010
surprise.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:03:00.470
Of course there was more going on behind the
scenes, and that’s where SCP-378 comes in.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:06.920
SCP-378 is an arthropod resembling an Amazonian
Giant Centipede, specifically one in the larval

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.040
stage.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:15.140
SCP-378 measures about 3 meters long, with
a thickness of 233 kilograms, and it possesses

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.730
otherwise ordinary biological features.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.620
Well, about as normal as an anomaly contained
by the SCP Foundation can get.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.560
On the dietary side, it eats like a normal
Centipede, consuming primarily other insects

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.670
and lichen.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.800
On the reproductive side, things are a bit
different.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.950
SCP-378 reproduces asexually, meaning that
it’s capable of generating offspring entirely

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:43.400
through its own biological functions, which
it excretes from its backside.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:51.200
A newly birthed instance, designated SCP-378-A,
resembles a fully grown adult Amazonian Giant

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.110
Centipede, right from birth.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:04:00.360
But dissection of these baby-pedes suggests
that this resemblance is superficial, as SCP-378-A’s

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:05.640
organ systems, beyond a primitive neural network,
are entirely nonexistent.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.830
These babies are essentially shell organisms,
intended to be used by SCP-378 - the host

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.010
centipede - as remote drones.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:20.560
And that’s how SCP-378 proves to be useful
to the Foundation at large.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.320
You see, SCP-378-A are endoparasites, meaning
that they’re organisms that live inside

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.980
other organisms.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:31.410
Like a tapeworm, but so, so much worse.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:39.190
SCP-378-A are used by SCP-378 to infect advanced
primates, such as ourselves, but also a whole

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:46.030
host of offshoots of humanity, such as the
//Homo ignotus//, also known as SCP-655,

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:54.900
and the //Gigantopithecus sapiens//, the common
sasquatch, which you might recognize as SCP-1000.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.560
SCP-378-A enters a host through an open orifice,
and after infection, the entity will integrate

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.780
itself with the host’s nervous system through
means that are poorly understood by the Foundation.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.810
This includes brain death of the host, and
extending SCP-378’s remote control through

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.510
its offspring and into the host itself.

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:19.700
Vital functions and sensory input remain unaffected,
meaning that the host still retains some semblance

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.330
of life function, just, you know, without
a brain or anything.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.660
They’re not there, they’re just puppets,
but you’d never be able to tell.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:32.830
When you think of someone being puppeted by
an anomalous parasite, you’d probably expect

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:34.300
them to act a little weird.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:38.540
Maybe they don’t speak right, or maybe they
start slithering on the floor or eating only

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:39.540
small lizards.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.280
But that’s not the case with victims of
SCP-378.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:50.550
No, once its offspring have infected a suitable
host, SCP-378 will actually do a pretty decent

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:55.130
job of integrating its victims back into their
respective social sphere.

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:59.760
This means maintaining all of their previous
relationships as best as possible, such as

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.320
keeping their jobs, and otherwise fitting
in and not standing above the crowd.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.080
You’d probably assume they prefer to keep
a low profile, mainly infecting hosts in small

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:13.070
towns and sleepy suburbs, but you’d be wrong
there too.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:20.350
SCP-378 loves the big city, places with high
population density, and a robust entertainment

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:21.350
scene.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:26.690
We’ll explain later, but SCP-378 differs
from ordinary insects in that its base instinct

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:32.130
isn’t just to infect and reproduce, it’s
to have fun, and the way it entertains itself

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:37.780
is by living the lives of people who otherwise
live busy, engaging lives, filled with plenty

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.300
of social interactivity and places to go.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:46.910
The Foundation has yet to discover the upper
limit of SCP-378, as the centipede has managed

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:51.600
to infect quite a number of hosts with its
offspring, which it all seems to maintain

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.710
simultaneously, without issue.

00:06:53.710 --> 00:07:00.370
Upon interrogation of a host, SCP-378 confessed
to the existence of twenty-six human hosts,

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:06.900
two instances of Guatemalan black howler monkeys,
and three instances of SCP-1000.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.330
Considering the complex social hierarchies
of monkeys, it's unsurprising that SCP-378

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:16.750
infected a few of them just to try them out,
on top of infecting a few sasquatches in the

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:23.930
form of SCP-1000, which SCP-378 conveniently
is immune to the inherent anomalous effects

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:24.930
of.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:29.540
But there’s no question as to which species
SCP-378 prefers.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:35.000
In fact, those other things it infected, according
to the host, were only acquired during periods

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.550
of heavy intoxication.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.919
See, the centipede enjoys getting a little
tipsy too.

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:42.780
You can’t have that in the amazon rainforest.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.530
As humanity is the dominant species on earth,
we’re the ones with the shiniest toys for

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:53.790
SCP-378 to play with, the most exciting lives,
filled with more pointless interaction and

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.310
labor than a bug would know what to do with.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:01.270
And that’s what makes SCP-378 so intriguing
for the Foundation.

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:06.780
It’s an intelligent parasite, whose only
motivation is to keep society running as normal

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:11.200
so it can slip behind a few pairs of human
eyes and see what it’s like in the world

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:13.540
it would never get to otherwise live in.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:19.060
Some of you are already thinking it, but SCP-378
is the perfect anomaly for the Foundation

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.520
to use against its foes and adversaries.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:28.889
And as we covered earlier, in the 1960s, the
Foundation had a lot to prove, mainly against

00:08:28.889 --> 00:08:32.000
the American Secure Containment Initiative.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.390
The Foundation knew to classify SCP-378 as
a thaumiel class object.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:40.960
It was promptly contained in an underground
terrarium filled with plenty of juicy lichen

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.280
and ants for SCP-378 to feed on.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.140
Of course, there was also a decontamination
chamber constructed adjacent to the regular

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:49.660
containment chamber.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:55.340
Any personnel who would go in would obviously
be at risk to an infection from SCP-378, and

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:59.900
we’re sure that living the crazy, exciting
life of a Foundation agent would’ve satisfied

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:04.860
the sociable bug, but the SCP Foundation wasn’t
taking any risks.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:09.300
As a result, anyone entering the chamber would
have to wear full-body protection, and if

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:15.310
they were found to be infected during the
decontamination process, they would be terminated.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:20.380
After discovery of SCP-378, the Foundation
immediately saw the potential in utilizing

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:23.120
the anomaly for espionage purposes.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:25.950
After all, it would be a perfect spy network.

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:30.560
Capable of fitting in and maintaining ordinary
roles while also communicating with SCP-378,

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:34.880
who would feed information to the Foundation
through another host.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.980
The impeccable precision of SCP-378’s maintenance
of its hosts' social personas would make the

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:44.930
entity almost impenetrable to sniff out in
the field, even for trained agents who deal

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:46.870
with the anomalous.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:51.650
SCP-378’s initial psychological evaluation
was performed by the well-known Foundation

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:54.140
psychologist, Dr. Simon Glass.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:10:00.200
Dr. Glass found that SCP-378 was obviously
unique among arthropods, and possessed either

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:06.110
human levels of sapience or at the very least,
the ability to emulate its hosts’ intellectual

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:07.110
faculties.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:14.110
Regardless, the anomaly was intelligent, but
a point of interest for Dr. Glass was SCP-378’s

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:15.230
relationship with its hosts.

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:21.210
It maintained a constant, consistent sense
of identity across multiple hosts, but interviews

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:27.500
with SCP-378 revealed that it was fully aware
that it was not those hosts in actuality.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:31.290
SCP-378 was essentially roleplaying various
human identities.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:36.240
It wasn’t just easy for the Foundation to
figure out that the centipede enjoyed masquerading

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:40.800
as its hosts, it pretty much spelled it out
with giant letters.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.870
SCP-378’s hosts rarely interact with or
send information directly back to SCP-378

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:52.990
or fellow hosts, because SCP-378 is in full
control at all times.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:58.339
This is literally just a game for SCP-378,
a source of entertainment.

00:10:58.339 --> 00:11:02.400
And if one of the hosts came under duress
or was at-risk of being found out by those

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:08.240
in its life, SCP-378 could easily abandon
the host persona, which it had no issues with

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:09.240
doing.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:14.800
Dr. Glass found that host behavior was largely
unique to each instance, and extroverted personalities

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:17.980
were a favorite for SCP-378.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:24.030
Hosts rarely isolate themselves, except to
sleep or use the bathroom, and SCP-378 appeared

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:29.050
to take equal enthusiasm in both stressful
and pleasant situations.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:34.750
After all, this was all a big roleplaying
session for it, SCP-378 appreciated some excitement

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:36.190
in the lives of its subjects.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:39.800
In a way, this is like an infinite source
of entertainment.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:44.190
You could live as many lives as you wanted
at once, have almost no attachment to any

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:48.390
consequences, because you’re a bug who has
no stake in the real world, and you get to

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:53.540
experience things no other member of your
species ever has or ever will.

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:57.400
SCP-378 is really living the dream.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.500
Even to another human, this sounds pretty
fun.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:06.270
We only get to live one life, SCP-378 gets
to live as many as it wants.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:10.680
But Dr. Glass found another interesting fact
about SCP-378.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.710
It was attached to a certain identity.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:18.550
Lisa Martin, a 33 year old Mexican-American
female employee at Digiannantono’s Pies,

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:23.300
a pizza chain located in Staten Island, New
York.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:29.440
Dr. Glass was a bit unsure as to why SCP-378
enjoyed Miss Martin so much, but it was clear

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:34.140
from the way it talked about her that this
was its favorite persona.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:38.170
And Dr. Glass could see why the more he studied
Martin’s routine.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:43.790
It was the perfect mixture of business, idleness,
routine labor, and general engagement for

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.640
SCP-378 to experience.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.680
Lisa Martin’s routine was static.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:54.790
On every day except Saturday, from 8 AM to
6 PM, Lisa Martin would show up to work at

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.450
Digiannantono’s Pies.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:13:01.250
After she finished work, from 6 PM to 11 PM,
Martin would engage in maintenance of one

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:04.560
of seventeen rooftop gardens across New York
City.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:09.690
Of these gardens, Glass discovered that thirteen
are maintained by volunteer organizations,

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:12.279
twelve of which Martin was not a part of.

00:13:12.279 --> 00:13:18.310
On Saturdays, from 8 AM to 11 PM, Martin alternated
between socializing with a collection of friends

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:22.310
and coworkers, and playing piano for various
high-end bars.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:27.300
On all days, from 11 PM to 12 AM, Martin would
shower and then prepare for bed.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.800
She would sleep from 12 AM to 7 AM, and in
the morning, she’d get back up to do it

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:33.000
all over again.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:37.320
When you see a person’s schedule laid out
like this, everything seems a bit more routine

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.040
and less exciting, huh?

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:46.520
It’s not an exciting life laid out on paper,
but SCP-378 loved it, for whatever reasons.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:50.330
Attempts to interrupt Lisa Martin’s routine
were unilaterally met with unusual levels

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:56.839
of hostility from the bug and its hosts, and
in the event of Lisa Martin’s death, SCP-378

00:13:56.839 --> 00:13:59.770
would happily direct another host to assume
her identity.

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:02.290
Yeah, we’re not sure either.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:05.100
Regardless, SCP-378 had a hobby.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:08.370
Many hobbies, but Lisa Martin was special.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:13.240
Dr. Glass’s evaluation proved to be popular
with the upper ranks of the Foundation, who,

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:18.820
due to SCP-378’s intelligence and awareness
of human sentience, could prove to be useful

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:21.320
to the overall mission of the Foundation.

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:26.620
As we said before, in 1963, the Foundation
had to contend with a number of powerful,

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:28.450
adjacent organizations.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:33.190
The Global Occult Coalition, with their destructive
tendencies and the backing of the world’s

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:34.190
governments.

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:38.529
GRU-Division “P”, the Soviet Union’s
anomalous research military unit.

00:14:38.529 --> 00:14:43.360
The remnants of the American Secure Containment
Initiative, who, while not as prominent or

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:48.910
powerful as they used to be, still remained
an organization capable of deterring the Foundation.

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:53.690
As a result, the Foundation looked into applying
SCP-378 through practical means.

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:58.230
Dr. Glass’s work importance couldn’t be
understated, but the prevailing theory was

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:04.860
that SCP-378 did not understand the significance
of social dynamics in regards to hierarchy

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:06.410
and social capital.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:09.950
This could be observed in its strange defense
of Lisa Martin.

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:15.209
Several of SCP-378’s hosts held positions
of power, two of which, David Lockheed and

00:15:15.209 --> 00:15:20.200
Alfonso Leoz, were even employed by anomalous
organizations, and beyond the reach of the

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:23.360
Foundation’s then-capacity to contain.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:29.500
Even holding these powerful positions, SCP-378
displayed a willingness to sacrifice the hosts

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:36.110
to defend, replace, or otherwise maintain
Lisa Martin, its favorite host.

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:39.209
It was here where the Foundation saw an opportunity.

00:15:39.209 --> 00:15:44.269
If they could use SCP-378’s favoritism of
Martin to get it to do what they wanted, their

00:15:44.269 --> 00:15:48.390
dream-spy network had a chance of manifesting
into reality.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:54.150
David Lockheed, one of SCP-378’s hosts,
was a low-level employee of the ASCI, but

00:15:54.150 --> 00:16:00.779
if the Foundation was able to persuade SCP-378,
using Martin’s safety as collateral, Lockheed

00:16:00.779 --> 00:16:05.140
could rise through the ranks of the organization
into a position where the Foundation needed

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:06.140
a puppet.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:10.290
Second, they realized that Martin herself
could prove to be a useful asset.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:14.520
The Foundation was always looking for new
ways to conduct reconnaissance, and pizza

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:19.540
parlors were an ideal meeting spot and espionage
center, if they could be kept from the public

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:20.540
eye.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:24.100
Martin’s place of employment would soon
be converted from your average Staten Island

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:29.370
pizza parlor to one of the Foundation’s
very own Spicy Crust Pizza chains.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.470
See what they did there?

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:32.110
Spicy Crust Pizza?

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:33.230
SCP?

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.540
They’re so clever.

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:40.660
Regardless, the use of SCP-378’s anomalous
abilities to defend Foundation operations

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:45.660
in the United States seemed like a useful
prospect, and the idea was put to a vote by

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:47.360
the Overseer Council.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:52.420
It passed, with only minor detration, under
the name of the “Kraken Protocol”.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:57.940
As a result, SCP-378’s containment procedures
were updated, and containment of the anomaly

00:16:57.940 --> 00:17:00.899
would be focused on 3 primary components.

00:17:00.899 --> 00:17:08.379
The first host, SCP-378-1, was held in the
Area-19 barracks of the SCP Foundation.

00:17:08.379 --> 00:17:13.069
-1 was employed as a maintenance technician
with a low security clearance, and in the

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:18.899
case of death, a brain-dead or comatose reserve
personnel would be elected to replace it.

00:17:18.899 --> 00:17:25.140
SCP-378-1 served as the Foundation’s primary
means of communication with SCP-378.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:30.169
After all, they needed a channel to speak
directly to the bug in charge, and -1 was

00:17:30.169 --> 00:17:32.510
their way of doing so.

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:38.460
SCP-378-2 was David Lockheed, an average employee
of the American Secure Containment Initiative

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:40.710
working in their clerical sector.

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:45.940
Lockheed was tasked with sabotaging ASCI operations
for the Foundation, as well as collecting

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:49.340
information in the Foundation’s interests.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:55.830
SCP-378-2 was a little more difficult to replace
than the average SCP-378 host, and was required

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:58.700
to follow a strict health and exercise regimen.

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:02.739
If something were to happen to Lockheed, there’s
no telling if the Foundation would be able

00:18:02.739 --> 00:18:06.639
to replace him, and the chances of that happening
were low.

00:18:06.639 --> 00:18:11.999
Lockheed was the perfect spy, with an established
rapport with the ASCI going back at least

00:18:11.999 --> 00:18:16.950
a decade, and access to information the Foundation
would use to outsmart the Initiative at every

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:17.950
turn.

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:23.249
It shouldn’t come as a surprise to you that
the ASCI’s days were numbered, and eventually,

00:18:23.249 --> 00:18:26.100
they closed their doors entirely.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:31.370
SCP-378-3 was Lisa Martin, who worked at Spicy
Crust Pizza in Staten Island.

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:36.091
Martin’s purpose was to maintain a network
for Foundation operatives in New York, and

00:18:36.091 --> 00:18:39.929
host a place where they could congregate and
meet without issue.

00:18:39.929 --> 00:18:45.230
On top of that, Martin was still SCP-378’s
favorite persona, and the entire operation

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:49.289
hinged on SCP-378 having Martin available
to it.

00:18:49.289 --> 00:18:54.309
As a result, Lisa Martin would have to be
replaced as soon as possible if anything were

00:18:54.309 --> 00:18:55.730
to happen to her.

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:58.989
And with that, the Kraken Protocol was in
place.

00:18:58.989 --> 00:19:04.029
Each instance formed a part of the Foundation’s
information-gathering campaign, and was outfitted

00:19:04.029 --> 00:19:07.080
with a tracking device and an audio recorder.

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:11.529
Every week, embedded agents stationed near
each host were to evaluate the health and

00:19:11.529 --> 00:19:15.169
integrity of them and the equipment they had
inside them.

00:19:15.169 --> 00:19:21.600
If this initial rollout was a success, the
Foundation would continue to utilize SCP-378-A

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.110
in further infiltration of its enemies.

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:27.499
But the Foundation would have to see how things
played out first.

00:19:27.499 --> 00:19:32.340
Less than two years later, Lockheed had retrieved
significant information for the Foundation

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:35.049
and the Kraken Protocol proved to be a success.

00:19:35.049 --> 00:19:41.679
A new site, Site-56, located near prominent
anomalous locations, was about to be constructed.

00:19:41.679 --> 00:19:47.600
SCP-378 seemed excited at the prospects of
a change in scenery and more hosts to infiltrate,

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:51.679
but there was also something the Foundation
could never have expected.

00:19:51.679 --> 00:19:57.590
In an attempt to gain a new host for SCP-378
to control, the Foundation came across something

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:58.590
disturbing.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:02.639
Their target was an agent of the FBI’s “Unusual
Incident Unit”.

00:20:02.639 --> 00:20:08.320
After drugging him and bringing him back to
the Foundation in order to receive an SCP-378-A

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:13.100
instance inside him, SCP-378 informed the
Foundation that it couldn’t establish a

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:14.739
connection with the agent.

00:20:14.739 --> 00:20:16.669
The personnel were confused.

00:20:16.669 --> 00:20:22.450
The agent was still alive, and SCP-378 never
had any difficulty in connecting with hosts

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:23.450
before.

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:27.330
They did a number of tests on the agent, and
tried to figure out what went wrong.

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:31.961
That’s when the Foundation discovered that
there was a different centipede inside the

00:20:31.961 --> 00:20:37.070
agent’s head, where the SCP-378-A instance
would typically be.

00:20:37.070 --> 00:20:43.730
It looked like the Foundation had a much bigger
issue on their hand than they ever had realized.

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:48.909
Now go check out “SCP-213 - Anti-Matter
Parasite” and “Reconstructive Maggots

00:20:48.909 --> 00:20:54.379
- SCP-726” for more SCPs that tend to be
a bit anomalously clingy!

